#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105659 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b8cf2b4-0b4f-513b-a5dc-f582c973e098" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable for Some Value"}] :question/id #uuid "9bdb609d-4000-5ee3-9267-71ed6a8dd140" :question/math-academy-id "e-7392" :question/problem "It is known that $75\\%$ of students pass a particular exam. If $5$ students who took the exam are randomly selected, what is the probability that $3$ of them passed the exam? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of students that passed the exam.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=5$ students\n\n- the probability that each student passed the exam is $p=75\\%=0.75$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(5,0.75).$ We want to find the probability that $x=3$ of the students passed the exam.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3})(0.75)^{3}(1-0.75)^{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{3!(5-3)!}(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =10(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =0.264\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105660 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce428740-1268-5071-9a45-69cdc07aea64" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable Over an Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "cbc5bcb5-2c64-5895-ba1c-cc6d86207667" :question/math-academy-id "e-7393" :question/problem "A standard fair die is rolled $8$ times. What is the probability of rolling an even number at least $7$ times? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times we get an even number.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ rolls\n\n- the probability of rolling an even number on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\dfrac{3}{6} = 0.5\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(8,0.5).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.5)^{x}(1-0.5)^{8-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of rolling an even number at least $7$ times. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 7) & =P(X=7)+P(X=8) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{7})(0.5)^{7}(0.5)^{8-7}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{8})(0.5)^{8}(0.5)^{8-8} \\\\  & =8(0.5)^{7}(0.5)+1(0.5)^{8} \\\\  & =0.035\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105661 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "1fab59f6-f640-5533-9bff-4946dfd71f77" :question/math-academy-id "e-7394" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The ages of $20$ college professors\n\n- The number of questions answered correctly by a student in a $12$-question exam\n\n- The heights of $10$ elementary school children" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The ages cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of questions answered in a $12$- question exam can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $12$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The heights cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105664 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d73da0e7-b787-5da3-8317-197e6d63d0ae" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Distribution Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/id #uuid "b4cfa71b-69f8-5cdb-b3c9-6dabdb32e264" :question/math-academy-id "e-7444" :question/problem "In a random card game, there is a $40\\%$ chance of winning a game. If the player plays $12$ games, what is the probability that he wins at least $2$ of the games? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of games that the player wins.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ games\n\n- the probability of winning each game is $p=40\\%=0.4$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(12,0.4).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x})(0.4)^{x}(1-0.4)^{12-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{12-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the player wins at least 2 of the games. This corresponds to $P(X \\geq 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 2) = P(X=2) + P(X=3) + \\ldots + P(X=12)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{12-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{12-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.6)^{12}+12(0.4)(0.6)^{11}] \\\\  & =1-0.019\\,591 \\\\  & =0.980\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b8cf2b4-0b4f-513b-a5dc-f582c973e098" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable for Some Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3b70e6c-57cb-5df5-9660-163b11755683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.159" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105221} {:answer/id #uuid "ce36c17a-038d-576e-8e33-ff32227e998d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.257" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105222} {:answer/id #uuid "100c026d-f05b-5aa4-9711-3cc3ac9b5eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.092" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105223} {:answer/id #uuid "4a3d6bbd-1871-5d55-8d51-96e54c8a82ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.688" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105224} {:answer/id #uuid "78494c48-24d8-59e5-925d-f6938421fa76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105225}] :answer-field/id #uuid "329b62cb-c9ff-58c9-b520-1cfc155601f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105220}] :question/id #uuid "7bd6c9ff-00f9-5185-b985-9d9007df87dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-84050" :question/problem "A softball team has a $70%$ chance of winning each game in a $10$-game season. What is the probability that the team wins $9$ of the $10$ games? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cc945cd-e0a6-5e86-adc0-7a5446b2a41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355050} {:answer/id #uuid "946e3569-88e7-5e1b-88fa-a93871220bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355971} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d1e7c0-f30e-5e03-ba20-ced2f5de00e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356229} {:answer/id #uuid "d39b7d0b-0005-5346-9793-d0d086cf68b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356513} {:answer/id #uuid "f03bc8d1-6109-574a-817b-84faa4cb038b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1d1e7c0-f30e-5e03-ba20-ced2f5de00e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356229} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd6b99f6-a1a6-5460-bc79-7d630213eba0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3640d184-7917-5cae-8823-08846078aa32" :question/math-academy-id "q-89354" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of baskets made by a basketball player attempting three free throws if the probability of scoring any free throw is $65%$\n- The number of defective items in a random sample of $100$ items produced by a machine\n- The number of views of a video on a popular video channel" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of baskets in $3$ throws can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $3$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of defective items in a random sample of $100$ items can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $100$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of views cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b8cf2b4-0b4f-513b-a5dc-f582c973e098" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Binomial Random Variable for Some Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc28274-a70b-5d5e-bb19-cf673ed6833a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.158" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355341} {:answer/id #uuid "4e445193-6d9a-5bd8-850e-4bcde5fe10bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.136" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355358} {:answer/id #uuid "697a72b6-5f3d-5c77-9de6-ab9f53145994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.089" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355593} {:answer/id #uuid "a57fb1fa-437e-53a3-8f89-114d427fe1b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356132} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e754f5-15e5-5c3d-9939-e1d2372af236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a57fb1fa-437e-53a3-8f89-114d427fe1b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356132} :answer-field/id #uuid "aad9ad01-2ce3-5873-a99b-0a674040b68d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16573e53-10fd-55fd-9ec2-393558355143" :question/math-academy-id "q-89366" :question/problem "A spinner with three regions is designed so that the probability it lands on red is $\\frac{3}{10},$ the probability it lands on blue is $\\frac{2}{5},$ and on green $\\frac{3}{10}.$ What is the probability that the spinner lands on red or green exactly $5$ times if it is spun $12$ times? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the spinner lands on red or green.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ spins\n\n- the probability of landing on red or green on any spin is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(12,\\frac{3}{5}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=5$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{5}){(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})}^{12-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{12!}{5!(12-5)!}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{7} \\\\  & =792{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{7} \\\\  & =0.101\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}]}
